- by Kerry Laidlaw 09/23/86*        69. Fixed a bug in the dispatcher that trashes screen when*            executed with the prefix set to nil.*            Previously when an error occured when the pathname*            was checked, the message at the top of the screen*            was redisplayed. Now we are jumping so the message*            is not redisplayed.  It saves us 2 bytes....*             (File:SEL)** Version 1.3   - by Mike Askins, Fern Bachman - 10/17/86*        70. The code that resets the phase lines for Disk //s has been*            changed so that the phase clearing is done with a load, not*            a store.  Stores to even numbered locations on the disk*            hardware cause data bus contention which is just not cool.*            The routine has been changed to access all 8 even locations*            which not only clears the phases but forces read mode (as*            well as 1st drive and motor off) on entry to the disk code.*            DOS used to do this and ProDOS did not.  If for some reason*            (copy protection, runaway programs) l7 was left high on*            entry to the disk routines, and the disk routines checked*            write protect with l6 high:  BINGO -- we're in write mode.*            Forcing read mode initially leaves less to chance.*            to assemble ProDOS8 with cross reference tables.*        71. The file MLI was changed to allow ASM816 version 1.4.2 to*            allow for cross reference listings to be generated.*            Assembling the file MLI generates ProDOS8 without listings.*            Assembling the file L.MLI generates PorDOS8 with a listing.*            NOTE: A blank RAM disk card called /RAM1 must be installed*                  in the system to cross references to be generated.*        72. The 'date' directive was removed in the file DEVSRCH*            and an ASC 'xx-xxx-xx' directive replaced it.** Version 1.3   - by Ray Chiang   11/07/86*        73. In reference to rev notes 41 and 49, index blocks now*            have their contents flipped when the file is deleted*            and their contents are fully zeroed when EOF cutback*            occurs.  This technique allows scavenging programs to*            possibly recover files that were accidently deleted.*            Also the possibility of unclaimed blocks 'looking'*            like valid index blocks is reduced.  Previous to this*            revision, whole index blocks from tree files were*            being released back into the disk.*            (files: DESTROY,DETREE,WRKSPACE)** Version 1.3   - by Fern Bachman 11/17/86*        74. The SmartPort interface card (our Liron interface card)*            does not set up its device chain unless a ProDOS call*            is made to it.  Therefore in DEVSRCH where Mike makes*            SmartPort calls the card is not setup yet so it returns*            00 devices in its chain.  This results in the card/drives*            never being seen by ProDOS.  To fix that we will now make*            a ProDOS status call before SmartPort status calls.  This*            should guarantee valid device counts.*            (file: DEVSRCH)** Version 1.4   - by Ray Chiang/Fern Bachman 02/03/87*        75. Fix illegal 65C02 instruction in 6502 code.  ie change*            the BRA to a BEQ instruction.** Version 1.4   - by Mike Askins/Fern Bachman 02/12/87*        76. Changed RSETPHSE routine to only clear the phase lines.*            And we clear Q7 so no writes can accidently occur.** Version 1.4  - by Ray Chiang/Mike Askins 03/23/87*        77. Simple programming error in fix 76 .** Version 1.4 - Ray Chiang 04/17/87*        78. No changes, released to SCM** Version 1.5d1 - Grady Ward 2 Sep 87*        79.  Fixed overrun problem in READ.WRITE *              Also, changed p16 patch addresses**        80.  Made call to DEVMGR surrounded by NOP's so that*              it can be patched by p16 and pquit more easily*              (for both cache and AppleTalk booting)** Version 1.5d7 - Grady Ward 20 Nov 87 Summary of Changes*        81.  Change to READ.WRITE in tstwprot to disable interrupts*              if going to use DMGR (Caching)**        82.  P8 code has been extensively moved about to make room*              for patches to DMGR dispatch (approximately 14 bytes *              were needed for AppleTalk booting patches caching patches)** Version 1.5d8 4-Dec-87	Grady Ward**        83.  Change some asc strings, like "system" uppercase.** Version 1.5d11 20-Jan-88	Grady Ward**        84.  Took out $42 generic AppleTalk Call** Version 1.5b2 16-Feb-88	Grady Ward**        85.  Restored asl mliactv to lsr in "exitmli"**  NOTE:	Investigation of this change by Tim Harrington and myself*	leads us to believe that the rationale for performing an*	lsr on the "mliactiv" byte is to force the high bit to zero*	unconditionally (note that the code is implemented so that*	only the HIGH BIT of that byte serves as the flag to*	indicate that an mli call is in progress when it is high,*	and any documentation which states that the zero-value of*	the entire byte serves as the busy flag is incorrect at*	this time).  The reason for always forcing the busy flag*	off, rather than restoring a previous value by use of an*	asl instruction, is that the quit code never clears the*	busy flag explicitly, and if control is passed to another*	application by means of a quit call, that application will*	inherit an environment in which the busy flag in set when*	it should be clear; the quit call itself makes mli calls*	(with the busy flag high--this is possible because the mli*	never in fact checks the state of the busy flag at any time)*	and as a side effect, the busy flag gets cleared when those*	calls are completed because an lsr instruction forces the*	high bit low.**	This situation was a point of concern at this time because*	the worstation software, PFI, vectors itself in as a shell*	around P8, and is itself non-re-entrant.  The hope was that*	the lsr could be changed back, again, to an asl in order to*	allow "nested" busy conditions and assure that interrupt*	routines would never re-enter PFI even if they were to gain*	control during the time window between the completion of an*	mli call and the completion of the PFI call which was "parent"*	to that mli call.**	The actual solution that was implemented is described in*	revision note #100.*	(Monte Benaresh -- April 1989)** Version 1.5b3 1-Mar-88	Grady Ward**        86. Clear ESC in keyboard if booting on Apple //c*                ("slow" down for Zip Chip)** Version 1.6 14-Jun-88		Grady Ward**        87. Set up parallel pfixptr to fix*                AppleTalk PFI misinterpretation*                of two's comp prefix pointer** Version 1.7 22-July-88	Grady Ward**        88.  When setting eof backwards, make sure*                 volumes bitmaps are counted before inc*                 free blocks**        89.  When installing ramdisk, make sure slot3rom*                 is preserved rather than overwritten**        90.  In system death, check "cortflag" rather than*                 "cortland" to ascertain what system we're on**        91.  Waited to restore slot3 firmware until just before*                  gquit is called at jspare**        92.  Now permit invisible bit to be set with set file info*                  (bit 2 of d.attr byte)** Version 1.8d1 01-Jan-89	Grady Ward**        93.	Converted to MPW MAX assembler syntax**        94.	Reworked conversion to preserve arrangement of code*	as in v1.7.  Added vector to calldisp so that GQuit*	can find it without having to hard-code that address.*	This vector appears as a dc.w near the end of PROCTHREE.*	(Grady Ward / Monte Benaresh -- December 1988)**        95.	Fixed a problem where the sequence which destroys a file*	aborts immediately without releasing the file's directory*	entry if an I/O error occurs during deallocation of*	blocks.  This situation leaves a directory entry for a*	file which may be "partially deleted," i.e., some of the*	entries in the file's index blocks may have been flipped.*	If a user tries to delete the file again (after receiving*	the I/O error) then the destroy code reads the partially*	flipped index blocks and starts deallocating the wrong*	blocks from the bitmap.  Obviously, this process can*	ruin the integrity of the disk.**	The fix causes the destroy sequence to mark the file's*	entry as deleted even if an error occurs at any time*	after the index blocks have been touched.  This remedy*	was chosen given the restriction that almost no*	bytes were available for expansion of the code.  With*	the fix in place, the set of circumstances which*	invoke the problem will at worst leave the disk with*	blocks marked in the bitmap as used which belong to*	no existing file, and stray index blocks which may*	be partially flipped.  The user will still receive an*	I/O error, but the file will then be absent from the*	directory, and a subsequent attempt to delete the same*	file will produce a "file not found" error (as would*	normally be expected).**	The implementation presumes a "critical point" in the*	code sequence after which the file's directory entry*	must be marked as deleted even if any operations*	fail after that point (this point is marked in the*	listing).  Since the code was designed to branch*	whenever an error occurs, it was possible to change all*	such branches (BCS) to point to the code which marks the*	file as deleted in the directory.  This code is at the*	label "zap_entry" and saves the accumulator (which*	contains an error code or 0 if no error) onto the stack.*	It then marks the memory image of the directory entry*	as deleted and does a JSR to the routine which writes*	the memory image out to the disk.  The code used to*	simply jump to that routine because it was the last*	task in the sequence, but the JSR is now necessary so*	that the code which was pushed onto the stack can*	be restored before returning to the user.  The ensuing*	operation ensure that if either an error code was pushed*	onto the stack or an error just occured when updating*	the directory, some error will be returned (the more*	recent code if both conditions are true).**	Note that a "jsr upbmap" instruction was moved from after*	the label "dst1" to its present position because no call*	to a routine which might fail can occur after the "zap_entry"*	label.  This move should be of no consequence.**	The addition of the fix widened a branch that appears before*	the label "dsdir3."  This motivated the inclusion of an*	additional "relay" branch at "go_dstlast."**	In order to accomodate the fix (and the extra branch)*	several places in the code were "crunched" (in fact, the*	branch mentioned above happens to appear in such a section).*	A couple of CLC's before RTS's were commented out after*	this engineer determined that they were redundant.  These*	are at the labels "dsapdone" and "dseedone."*	(Monte Benaresh -- January 1989)**        96.	Fixed a bug where the ONLINE call didn't ignore the unused*	low nybble of the unit_num parameter when deciding how*	many bytes to zero out in the user buffer before filling it*	in with the requested data.  Thus, if the user passed a*	unit_num of, say, $01, P8 would zero out only the first 16*	bytes of the user buffer, and then fill the buffer with up*	to 256 bytes of data.  This is a problem because the user*	was now left without the 2 null bytes which indicate the*	end of the list when the list does not completely fill the*	buffer.**	The fix performs the mask and stores the result in "devnum"*	for later reference and for the online1 subroutine in case*	the unit_num is non-zero.  The online1 subroutine no longer*	begins with "sta devnum."  For this reason, the "jsr*	online1" just before the label "olin4" is now preceded by*	"lda devnum."**	The instructions at "olin1" used to be:**		lda #1*		sta cbytes+1**	but have been replaced with simply "inc cbytes+1" since*	"cbytes+1" was initialized to zero at the start of the*	"online" sequence.  This was done to make room for the fix.*	(Monte Benaresh -- January 1989)**        97.	P8 now sets the video firmware "mode" byte ($4FB) to 0*	and calls SETVID $FE93 again if running on a GS (Cortland).*	This guarantees that the blinking "checkerboard" cursor*	will be set up correctly when BASIC (or any other SYSTEM*	program) gets control.  Before this change, the steady*	solid block was appearing as the cursor if BASIC.SYSTEM*	was launched from the finder under System Disk 4.1d10.*	The fix appears between "savemach" and "itsp8" in*	a section that only gets executed on a Cortland.*	(Monte Benaresh -- February 1989)**        98.	Added the "GS/OS compatibility" byte at $BFFA.*	This byte appears at $50FA when P8 is loaded at $2000*	(the usual load address).  GQuit now checks the value of*	this byte after loading P8 to assure functional*	compatibility.  The present value there is $01, and in*	recent versions of P8 it has been $00.  For early versions,*	there is code (which is to be relocated during P8's*	startup sequence) at $50FA, but the actual values will not*	interfere with this scheme until future compatibility*	values climb very high ($2B).**	Previously, GQuit checked the ASCII values of the*	characters in the splash screen message, which required*	GQuit to know their location.  The new scheme allows the*	splash screen message to move (as it did for rev #97) and*	also allows a compatible release of P8 to work with GQuit*	even if P8's version number changes.*	(Monte Benaresh -- February/March 1989)**       98a.	Since some applications depend on the value of the*	"kbakver" byte at $BFFE, which was the original choice*	for the location of the "GS/OS compatibility" byte, its*	value must be preserved as $00.  It is for this reason*	that the GS/OS compatibility byte was moved to $BFFA.**        99.	Removed the backward compatibility check that was being*	performed when the user specified a path containing a*	subdirectory.  The version and min_version fields are now*	used by the GS/OS ProDOS File System Translator to store*	upper/lower case patterns for names, so this test now*	fails if performed on a subdirectory whose name includes*	lower case characters as supported by the ProDOS FST.*	Since the min_version field was never used in a meaningful*	way, the BNE instruction in the test (before the label*	tstpas0 in PROCTHREE) has been replaced by*	two NOP's, so the test can never fail.**	This code should actually be removed, as should all of*	the code which supports password protection, which was*	never functionally implemented.  Byte positions have been*	preserved by the use of NOP's because this change comes*	moments before the Beta release of System Disk 5.0, and*	the incompatibility risks associated with shuffling code*	(and recovering the space used by the bogus code) are*	being deferred until the next release.*	(Monte Benaresh -- March 1989)**       100.	No code change.  A restriction has been imposed which*	requires the entry point at the label "exit" on the global*	page to remain at $BFA0, and for the state of the stack on*	entry to that code to never change.  These restrictions are*	added to support PFI, the workstation software, which*	installs itself as a shell around P8.  PFI patches the code*	at "exit" and manipulates return addresses on the stack in*	order to assure that control never be passed to an interrupt*	routine which intended to make an mli call but noted that*	the "mli busy" flag (the HIGH BIT of the byte at "mliactv")*	was high, and thus "deferred" its own execution by use of*	the mechanism describe on page 108 of the ProDOS 8 Technical*	Reference Manual in section 6.2.1, "Interrupts During MLI*	Calls."  The problem involves the possibility of an*	interrupt routine gaining control just after completion of*	an mli call which was issued by PFI and before PFI was*	itself done with its level of processing.  In such a case*	the interrupt routine could then legally make a P8 call*	which passed control to PFI, thus effectively re-entering*	PFI.**	To solve this problem, PFI now patches the "exit" code*	and manipulates return addresses on the stack in such a*	way that causes a deferred interrupt routine to gain*	control only after PFI has finished its level of*	processing and is ready to handle a P8 call if the*	interrupt routine should issue one.*	(Monte Benaresh -- April 1989)**       101.	Expanded the size of the loader portion in order to make*	room for a GS QuickDraw patch.  The loader portion of*	the code is of predefined size and was previously $B00*	bytes long.  This fact is hard-coded in  this file,*	in the "ram.n file", and in the makefile, so expansion*	of this area is a non-trivial task that required some*	changes to be made by hand.  Those changes are logged in a*	document titled "Loader Code Expansion Notes."**	The patch, at label "InstallOSPatch," fixes a bug in the*	5.0 System Disk where QuickDraw makes GS/OSª calls without*	first checking that the System is installed in the machine.*	The patch installs code which is vectored into the GS/OSª*	entry points and returns an error for GS/OSª calls.  This*	fixes things because QuickDraw handles GS/OSª errors properly.**	The patch is called just before the label "itsp8" and*	should only get executed when running on a GS and when P8*	is being booted directly, i.e., not being installed by GQuit.*	The patch disables interrupts (for no specific reason, only*	as a precaution), switches the machine to native mode,*	allocates memory for the "real" GS/OS patch, copies that code*	into the allocated memory, sets up both GS/OSª entry points*	to point to their appropriate pieces of the code, restores*	the environment and then returns.**	Since these changes must be reflected in GQuit, the value*	of the GS/OSª compatibility byte on $BF00 page has been*	changed from $01 to $02.  Also, GQuit now checks the size*	of the P8 object file to ensure that it can find this byte*	in the first place.  The size of the P8 file is now $3D7D*	bytes.  The location of the GS/OS compatibility byte in*	the P8 object file has moved from $50FA to $51FA.**	Updated splash screen date text from "31-MAR-89"*	to "23-MAY-89".*	(Monte Benaresh -- May 1989)** Version 1.9d1 - 12 Jun 90 - Greg Branche**        102.	Bug fix to BRC #65472 - When displaying the message*	'** SYSTEM PROGRAM TOO LARGE **', the code was looping*	through the string intending to use the Y register as*	a counter.  It was actually using a 'dex' instruction*	instead of a 'dey'.  This has been corrected.**        103.	Bug fix to BRC #65473 - When loading the first .SYSTEM*	program found on disk, the code was making sure that*	the program was not too big by checking the mid byte*	of the file's EOF against the constant value $98.  This*	was limiting the size of the file that could be loaded*	to 38K when there's actually 39.75K available*	($BF00-$2000).  This has been corrected by changing the*	operand of the 'cmp' instruction to $9F.**        104.	Redundant EQU's have been removed from ProcOne to*	dispose of the assembler warnings.**        105.	Loading X and Y for the subroutine call to 'reloc' has*	been changed from absolute addressing to immediate*	addressing.  This was done to save some space and*	execution time (one less byte per load, plus the*	addresses don't need to be stored in a table anymore).*	This has been conditionally installed, though.  Only by*	setting the 'needroom' equate to 1 will the changes*	be made at assembly time.  Use the following line to*	set the label value correctly.needroom	equ 0**        106.	An enhanced version of the Quit handler has been*	added, along with the necessary logic to decide*	which version of the handler to use.  The enhanced*	version will automatically read a directory file*	and display all files with type $FF or $0F (applications*	or directories) for the user to select from.  The*	user is allowed to use the arrow keys to move  a*	selection cursor from name to name, RETURN to select*	a name, TAB to step through the attached devices, or*	ESC to close the current directory and step back one*	directory in the pathname.  When the user presses*	RETURN when the cursor is on a directory, that directory*	is opened and the files contained within that directory*	are displayed for selection.  If the selected file is*	an application, the code will load the file and transfer*	control to it.**	This enhanced handler is only installed on //e or newer*	machines that have an 80-column card installed.  Machines*	that don't have the necessary hardware to support the*	new handler will have the original QUIT handler installed.**        107.	During a cold boot, if a .SYSTEM program can't be found*	and the new quit code has been installed, the code*	now performs a simple _Quit call to invoke the new*	quit code.  This will allow the user to launch P8*	without requiring a .SYSTEM program on the disk, yet*	the user will still be able to select an application*	to execute.** Version 1.9B1 - 12 Jun 90 - Greg Branche*	Only a version number change.  (Sure was easy!)** Version 1.9   - 16 Jul 90 - Greg Branche*	Golden Master build.**	(Mods 110 & 111 performed 22 Aug 90 by Greg Branche)*	(changes to source code bracketed by ;*** gab *** and ;*** end ***)**       110.	Added support for second filename parameter when launching P8*	applications from the P16 environment.  This convention is described*	on page 88 of the ProDOS 8 Technical Reference Manual.  This is accomplished*	by defining an alternate version of the dispatcher code that get's copied*	to $D100-$D3ff in the second bank of the language card area of memory.*	This normally get's patched by GQuit anyway, so the remainder of the code*	might as well be used by GQuit to support the application launch.*******************************************************************